19:["$","main",null,{"className":"docs_main__7o2m6","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"docs_articleMeta__8zYHO","children":[["$","span",null,{"children":"LESSON 03 OF 5"}],["$","span",null,{"children":"Foundation"}]]}],["$","article",null,{"className":"docs_article__Ja_Vh","children":[["$","$1","0",{"children":[[["$","h1","h1-0",{"children":"Loss, parameters, and gradient descent"}],"\n",["$","p","p-0",{"children":"A model learns by changing numbers that influence its predictions. This lesson explains that loop with one small calculation. You do not need to implement an optimizer to review a model-backed feature, but you should know what was learned, what was chosen by an engineer, and why a low training error can mislead."}],"\n",["$","h2","h2-0",{"id":"a-model-is-a-function-with-adjustable-parameters","children":"A model is a function with adjustable parameters"}],"\n",["$","p","p-1",{"children":["For a simple numeric prediction, write ",["$","code","code-0",{"children":"prediction = weight × input + bias"}],". The ",["$","strong","strong-0",{"children":"weight"}]," and ",["$","strong","strong-1",{"children":"bias"}]," are parameters. Training changes them. The form of the function is the model family; its capacity determines which patterns it can represent. A ticket classifier uses more features and returns scores for billing, access, and technical queues, but it follows the same idea: inputs pass through a parameterized function to produce a prediction."]}],"\n",["$","p","p-2",{"children":["The ",["$","strong","strong-0",{"children":"loss"}]," measures how wrong a prediction is on an example. For a numeric target, squared error is ",["$","code","code-0",{"children":"(prediction − target)²"}],". For classification, a loss such as cross-entropy penalizes assigning low probability to the correct class. Loss is a training signal, not a complete product success metric. An average loss can hide errors on a small but important group."]}],"\n",["$","h2","h2-1",{"id":"follow-one-update","children":"Follow one update"}],"\n",["$","p","p-3",{"children":["Suppose ",["$","code","code-0",{"children":"input = 2"}],", ",["$","code","code-1",{"children":"target = 4"}],", ",["$","code","code-2",{"children":"weight = 1"}],", and ",["$","code","code-3",{"children":"bias = 0"}],". The prediction is ",["$","code","code-4",{"children":"2"}],"; squared error is ",["$","code","code-5",{"children":"4"}],". Holding the bias fixed, the gradient of this example's loss with respect to the weight is ",["$","code","code-6",{"children":"2 × (prediction − target) × input = −8"}],". With learning rate ",["$","code","code-7",{"children":"0.1"}],", one gradient step gives ",["$","code","code-8",{"children":"new weight = 1 − 0.1 × (−8) = 1.8"}],". The new prediction is ",["$","code","code-9",{"children":"3.6"}],"; squared error is ",["$","code","code-10",{"children":"0.16"}],"."]}],"\n",["$","p","p-4",{"children":["This is a teaching calculation for one example, not evidence that a model generalizes. In real training, updates use many examples, and a step that improves one can hurt another. The ",["$","strong","strong-0",{"children":"learning rate"}]," is a hyperparameter chosen before or during the run. If it is too large, updates can overshoot; if too small, progress can be slow. Other hyperparameters include regularization strength and model size."]}],"\n",["$","h2","h2-2",{"id":"diagram-parameter-updates-and-release-boundary","children":"Diagram: parameter updates and release boundary"}],"\n",["$","pre","pre-0",{"children":["$","code","code-0",{"className":"language-aosdf-diagram","children":"Training batch ──▶ predictions ──▶ loss against labels ──▶ gradients\n       ▲                                              │\n       └──────────── updated parameters ◀────────────┘\n                              │\n                     frozen, versioned model ──▶ inference\n"}]}],"\n","$L25","\n","$L26","\n","$L27","\n","$L28","\n","$L29","\n","$L2a","\n","$L2b","\n","$L2c","\n","$L2d","\n","$L2e","\n","$L2f","\n","$L30"],false]}]]}],"$L31"]}]
