2d:["$","ul","ul-0",{"children":["\n",["$","li","li-0",{"children":"Does a lower training loss prove that ticket routing improved for future tickets?"}],"\n",["$","li","li-1",{"children":"What is the difference between a model parameter and a learning rate?"}],"\n",["$","li","li-2",{"children":"Why should a retraining run create a new model version?"}],"\n"]}]
2e:["$","p","p-9",{"children":[["$","strong","strong-0",{"children":"Answer guide:"}]," with learning rate ",["$","code","code-0",{"children":"0.25"}],", weight becomes ",["$","code","code-1",{"children":"3"}],", prediction becomes ",["$","code","code-2",{"children":"6"}],", and squared error returns to ",["$","code","code-3",{"children":"4"}],"; the step overshot. A lower training loss proves only better fit to training examples. Parameters are updated from data; learning rate controls the update. Versioning supports comparison and rollback."]}]
